title: Genesis 5 – The Generations of Adam
description: A verse-by-verse study of Genesis 5: ten generations from Adam to Noah, the refrain "and he died," Enoch who walked with God, and a literal chronology.
output: lessons/genesis/genesis-5-generations-of-adam.html

=== Introduction
--- The Generations of Adam [title]
overline: Young Adult Sunday School • Genesis Series
heading: Genesis 5: The Generations of Adam
sub: From Creation to Noah • A Testimony in Years
refs: Genesis 5:1-32
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

--- The Book of the Generations
kicker: vv. 1–2
sub: In the Likeness of God
scripture: Genesis 5:1-2
p: Moses opens this chapter the same way he opens a genealogy anywhere in Scripture, with a “book,” a formal, deliberate record. This is not legend passed down loosely around a fire. It is a document, a toledoth, meant to be taken as literal history, the same way we take the record of names, dates, and reigns anywhere else in the Old Testament.

=== From Adam to Jared
--- Adam: The First Entry
kicker: vv. 3–5
sub: In His Own Likeness, After His Image
scripture: Genesis 5:3-5
p: Verse 1 said Adam was made in God’s likeness. Verse 3 says Seth was born in Adam’s likeness: fallen, mortal, sinful. That is the whole doctrine of the fall in one verse: what is passed down is not innocence but Adam’s own broken image. And then the refrain begins: and he died. God’s word to Adam in the garden was not a bluff.

--- Seth
kicker: vv. 6–8
sub: Nine Hundred Twelve Years, and He Died
scripture: Genesis 5:6-8

--- Enos
kicker: vv. 9–11
sub: Then Began Men to Call Upon the {Lord}
scripture: Genesis 5:9-11
p: Enos’s own birth was the moment [[Genesis 4:26|Gen. 4:26]] marks. With the third generation from Adam, public, corporate calling on the name of the {Lord} begins, a confession made with the mouth, standing alongside the blood faith of Abel.

--- Cainan
kicker: vv. 12–14
sub: Nine Hundred Ten Years, and He Died
scripture: Genesis 5:12-14

--- Mahalaleel
kicker: vv. 15–17
sub: Eight Hundred Ninety-Five Years, and He Died
scripture: Genesis 5:15-17

--- Jared
kicker: vv. 18–20
sub: Nine Hundred Sixty-Two Years, and He Died
scripture: Genesis 5:18-20
p: Jared lived longer than all but Methuselah, and he is the father of the one man in this chapter for whom the refrain never comes. Watch what happens in the very next entry.

=== The man who did not die
--- Enoch, the Seventh from Adam
kicker: vv. 21–24
sub: And Enoch Walked With God: And He Was Not; For God Took Him
scripture: Genesis 5:21-24 | mark: walked with God
p: Every other name in this chapter closes the same way: and he died. Enoch’s does not. He is the seventh from Adam, not to be confused with Cain’s son Enoch, third from Adam in the other line ([[Genesis 4:17|Gen. 4:17]]), and Scripture is careful to tell us this was no ordinary death. [[Hebrews 11:5|Heb. 11:5]] calls it a translation, by faith, and testifies that before it happened, he pleased God.

--- Hebrews 11 and Jude: Enoch’s Testimony
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 5:24
fig: Hebrews 11:5 | Hebrews 11:5 | Two things are named here: the mechanism (faith) and the reward (translation, so that he never faced death at all). Before it happened, his testimony was already recorded: he pleased God.
fig: Jude 14-15 | Jude 1:14-15 | Jude, writing near the end of the New Testament, quotes a sermon preached before the flood. Enoch’s prophecy of the Lord’s coming in judgment is the same hope every believer since has held: Christ’s return with His saints.

--- Hebrews 11 and Jude: Enoch’s Testimony (continued)
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 5:24
fig: John 8:51 | John 8:51 | What happened to Enoch bodily is the promise held spiritually for every believer in Christ: those who keep His word will never see death in the sense that matters, separation from God forever.
callout: Enoch is not a strange exception tucked into a genealogy. He is proof, planted in the earliest pages of Scripture, that walking with God by faith is a life death cannot ultimately touch.

=== From Methuselah to Noah
--- Methuselah
kicker: vv. 25–27
sub: The Oldest Man Who Ever Lived
scripture: Genesis 5:25-27
p: Nine hundred sixty-nine years: no man in Scripture, before or since, lived longer. Even the longest life this side of Eden still ends the same way every other life in this chapter ends. The refrain catches up to everyone but one.

--- Lamech
kicker: vv. 28–31
sub: This Same Shall Comfort Us
scripture: Genesis 5:28-31
p: This is a different Lamech than Cain’s descendant of [[Genesis 4:23-24|Genesis 4]] who boasted of vengeance seventy and sevenfold. This Lamech, of the line of Seth, names his son in hope, looking for relief from the curse on the ground pronounced back in [[Genesis 3:17|Genesis 3:17]]. He could not have known how, but Noah’s name proved prophetic the day the flood lifted and a new world began.

--- Noah
kicker: v. 32
sub: Shem, Ham, and Japheth
scripture: Genesis 5:32
p: Ten generations, one continuous line, from the dust of Eden to the three sons who would repopulate the whole earth after the flood. The chapter that opened “in the likeness of God” closes by naming the men through whom every nation on earth descends.

=== Names and the refrain
--- Hidden in the Hebrew: The Names Themselves
kicker: The Ten Names, In Order
sub: A Sentence Written Into the Genealogy
table: Name | Root Meaning
tr: Adam | Man
tr: Seth | Appointed
tr: Enosh (Enos) | Mortal
tr: Kenan (Cainan) | Sorrow
tr: Mahalalel | The Blessed God
tr: Jared | Shall come down
tr: Enoch | Teaching
tr: Methuselah | His death shall bring
tr: Lamech | The despairing
tr: Noah | Rest

--- The Gospel, Hidden in the Meaning of the Names
quote: Man is appointed mortal sorrow, but the Blessed God shall come down teaching that His death shall bring the despairing rest. | Read straight down the ten names
p: Read straight down the family tree, in Hebrew, the ten names carrying us from Adam to Noah string together into that sentence: the gospel itself, laid into a genealogy centuries before Christ came down, taught among us, died, and gave rest to the despairing. Whether every root is pressed with equal precision or not, the pattern is hard to miss: the God who inspired this list wastes nothing, not even the names.

--- And He Died: The Refrain of the Chapter
kicker: Eight Times Repeated
sub: The Wages of Sin, Written in a Genealogy
stat: 8 | times: and he died
center: They Lived, They Had Children, and They Died
p: Long-lived though they were, every man in this chapter but one lived, had sons and daughters, and died. Sons of God calling on the name of the {Lord} and sons of men who did not: all of them sinned, and all of them, in due time, died. This is not poetry or picture; it is the plain, literal accounting of years from creation to Noah. Taking the figurative as figurative and the literal as literal, these ages are given to us as literal history, the same rule of interpretation that lets us call Christ’s parables parables without turning His genealogies into parables too. [[Romans 5:12|Rom. 5:12]]

--- Romans 6:23: The Wages of Sin Is Death
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 5, “and he died”
fig: Romans 6:23 | Romans 6:23 | Genesis 5 is Romans 6:23 written out in eight literal case studies. Every long life in the chapter still ends exactly where Paul says sin’s wages lead.
fig: Romans 5:12 | Romans 5:12 | Adam’s sin did not stay Adam’s problem. It passed to every name on this genealogy, sons of God and sons of men alike, whether they called upon the name of the {Lord} or rejected His salvation.
callout: The refrain “and he died” is not filler between the numbers. It is the whole doctrine of sin and death, stated eight times over, so that the ninth time, when it doesn’t come, we notice.

=== Counting the years
--- Counting the Years: From Creation to Christ
kicker: Genesis 5 and 11 • A Literal Chronology
sub: About 2,008 Years, Adam to Abraham
table: Patriarch | Age at Next Birth
tr: Adam | 130
tr: Seth | 105
tr: Enos | 90
tr: Cainan | 70
tr: Mahalaleel | 65
tr: Jared | 162
tr: Enoch | 65
tr: Methuselah | 187
tr: Lamech | 182
tr: Noah (to Shem) | ~500
tr: **Adam to Shem** | **1,556 yrs**

--- Adam to Abraham
p: Following the given years of Genesis 5 forward, and then adding the generations of [[Genesis 11:10-32|Genesis 11:10–32]] from Shem down to Terah, father of Abraham, we arrive at roughly 2,008 years from Adam to Abraham. This isn’t a modern estimate imposed on the text; it’s simple addition of the years the text itself gives us.

--- Abraham to Christ
kicker: Matthew 1:17
sub: Fourteen Generations, Three Times Over
scripture: Matthew 1:17
p: Matthew closes the loop Genesis 5 opens. Between the literal years of Genesis and the counted generations of Matthew 1:17, Scripture hands us the recorded span from creation to Christ, inspired and preserved. Add the roughly two thousand years since, and the commonly accepted span places the earth at just slightly more than six thousand years old, give or take a century or two.

=== The pattern
--- A Pattern of Salvation in the Early Patriarchs
kicker: Adam → Abel → Seth and Enos → Enoch
sub: Knowledge, Blood, Confession, and a Walk With God
lead: Reading these early chapters together, a pattern for salvation emerges in the lives of these men:
point: Adam | Knows God his Creator, and knows his own sin.
point: Abel | Brings the blood of atonement, offered in faith for redemption.
point: Seth and Enos | Show that with the mouth, confession is made: calling upon the name of the {Lord}.
point: Enoch | Gives the testimony that he walked with God, that he lived separated, pleasing God.
p: Each of us passes through these same steps when we come to Christ and live for Him, an illustration God gave in His word, through the lives of these men, long before the cross.

--- Translated That He Should Not See Death
kicker: [[Hebrews 11:5|Hebrews 11:5]] • [[John 8:51|John 8:51]]
sub: A Prophet Who Preached, and a Promise to Every Believer
p: Enoch was more than a name in a list. He was a prophet. [[Jude 1:14-15|Jude 1:14–15]] preserves his preaching, a witness in the Spirit to Christ’s coming victory in the day of the Lord, given ages before the flood, let alone the cross. And what happened to him is the promise held out to every believer since: [[John 8:51]], “if a man keep my saying, he shall never see death.”

--- What Does “Translated” Mean?
kicker: A question worth sitting with • Genesis 5:24
fig: John 3:13 | John 3:13 | Jesus says plainly that no man has ascended to heaven except Himself. So what are we to make of Enoch being taken, and of Elijah’s whirlwind ascent in [[2 Kings 2:11|2 Kings 2]]? Scripture doesn’t fully resolve the question for us, and speculation runs quickly into Jewish fables and books that are not Scripture.
callout: What we can say plainly: Enoch walked with God, prophesied of Christ, and was pleasing to God, and he did not see death. Beyond that, Genesis leaves the mechanics a mystery, and we do well to leave them there too, drawing our application from what is written rather than from what isn’t.

--- Not Every Old Book Is Scripture
kicker: A caution
sub: The “Book of Enoch” Is Not Scripture
p: There are rabbit trails worth naming and leaving alone. Ancient Jewish fables and the pseudepigraphal “Book of Enoch” claim to fill in what Genesis leaves silent, but the book contradicts itself and contains plain fabrications. It is not Scripture, whatever curiosity it satisfies. What we can know, and should hold to, is simply this: Enoch walked with God, prophesied of Christ, and pleased God, and by faith, we’re meant to draw the same inspiration to live pleasing to God ourselves, in the Spirit, however our own days end.
